  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Nor'easter moves away today, leading to a dry and\nwarmer than normal stretch of weather through Thursday.\n\nThe low to our east is already filling nicely, leading to a big\nreduction in wind/gusts. Some places may go calm by morning but\nreturn to 5-10mph with no sig gusts in the fore-noon. Clouds\nwill be tough to get rid of today and should only start to break\nslowly, with much of the CWA staying cloudy tonight and Tues AM.\nBut, the subsidence under a big sfc high will help us improve on\nTues. Temps will max out in the 60s today. With the clouds still\nhanging around for much of the area, temps won't fall more than\n10-12F thru the night. We'll add 5-6F to the maxes for Tues and\nadd a few degs more Wed and Thurs, too. The dewpoints do inch up\neach day, too, and will be 55-65F on Thurs as maxes get to\n75-85F. Pretty summer-like. Probably not too much fog \ntonight/Tues AM, but the sky should be more- clear than cloudy \nTues PM and Wed AM. That should allow for some river valley fog.\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Cold front moves in late in the week.\n\nA cold front will approach from the Upper Great Lakes, slowing a\nbit as it moves through the region. Moisture will be pretty\nlimited as it moves through the CWA Thurs PM and Fri. NBM PoPs\n(60s NW, 30s SE) may actually be a little high. But, at this\nrange, it's probably not worth trying to tweak them. It looks \nlike the front will then get hung up and stretch west to east \nover VA. At that point, a surge of moisture associated with the \nremnants of Polo will move through the OH and TN Valleys. The\nmoisture will overrun the old front and likely bring rain and\ncooler temps for the weekend.",
